## Key JD insights

- **This is an AI-agent builder role, not a classic feature PM.** The core mission is to "design, build, and scale AI agents and automation workflows that teams genuinely rely on" using ChatGPT, Claude, n8n, Glean, Rovo, Zapier, Cursor "or equivalent". This is the single most direct match in Nick's whole pipeline to what he actually does day to day (PAI, Claude Code, agent orchestration, AccessBuddy built solo at small-team velocity).
- **Hands-on, full-lifecycle, commercial.** Discovery -> prototyping -> rollout -> iteration -> long-term optimisation, with explicit commercial framing (CRO, funnel optimisation, conversion, measurable outcomes). Rewards the eng+product+commercial blend, not pure management.
- **Stakeholder breadth across the business.** Growth, Content, Brand, Product, Engineering, design, analytics. Maps cleanly to Nick's multi-stakeholder alignment record (SLSA seven jurisdictions, LIMSOC operating model, Macquarie stakeholder-led delivery).
- **Fintech / regulated-adjacent.** Mortgages and property finance. Nick's ANZ and Macquarie regulated-finance background is a direct domain and cultural fit.
- **Explicitly portfolio-led hiring.** "We know great candidates don't always tick every box... we'd love to see what you've built." This rewards Nick's body of built work far more than a tick-box CV would.
- **Level is genuinely junior.** "Associate", "early in your product or technology career", a 6/12-month success ladder that tops out at "operating with increasing autonomy across more complex problem spaces". That is a growth-into-the-role framing, well below Nick's senior/lead level.

## Decision for Nick (the one real call)

This role is a **thesis bullseye and a level mismatch at the same time.**

- **Why it is bullseye:** it is the closest match in the pipeline to M1 (recognised leader in AI) and P3 (100x with AI, then show others). It is literally a paid mandate to build agentic AI systems that an org relies on, inside a high-growth fintech. The portfolio-led hiring line is built for someone with Nick's stack of built work.
- **Why the mismatch matters:** it is an **Associate PM** role pitched at early-career. Likely band ~$110k-$140k package (Melbourne APM, fintech), materially under the ~$180k-$200k runway target. Title is a step down from senior/lead.

Three ways to play it, pick one before this goes out:

1. **Apply, anchor up.** Treat the "Associate" label as the floor, not the ceiling. The JD's "increasing autonomy across more complex problem spaces" and "we'd love to see what you've built" leave room for a standout builder to be hired above-band or rapidly re-levelled. Lead the application with proof of agentic builds and let the level conversation happen live. Highest-upside, on-thesis, but risk of being screened as over-qualified or anchored to APM comp. **Recommended if the AI-builder identity outweighs the comp/title hit for this stretch.**
2. **Apply as a deliberate foot-in-door.** Accept the level/comp downgrade as the cost of getting inside a fintech doing exactly the AI work he wants, then grow. Fits the "bridge, keep working the pivot from inside" logic already in the career plan, but here the bridge is into AI-product rather than engineering. Only makes sense if the runway maths tolerates a sub-target salary.
3. **Park it.** If sub-$140k and an "Associate" title are disqualifying given runway to Aug 2, park with reason "Associate-level comp/title below runway target despite strong AI-agentic fit" and spend the throughput on senior-level AI roles (the Wesfarmers/Virgin/Jora tier).

My read: this is worth applying to under option 1, because the role is a rare exact match for the identity Nick is trying to build (AI leader) and Lendi's own language invites over-delivery. But the comp gap is real and his call, not mine. If he wants, clarify the band early via a recruiter touch before investing in the tailored package.

## Why resume-hybrid (not SWE, not Consulting)

The role sits dead-centre on the product/engineering/AI intersection. resume-hybrid is the only variant that carries product ownership, AI delivery, and hands-on building in one document:

- It leads with product framing and an AI-builder narrative, which is what an "Agentic APM" screens on.
- It keeps the ANZ generative AI work (Vertex/Gemini, AI-enabled search APIs, Claude Code early trial, responsible-AI guardrails) - the headline professional AI credential.
- It carries AccessBuddy as a solo, AI-leveraged, full-stack build, which is the closest thing on the CV to "AI agents and workflows teams rely on".

resume-swe would under-sell the product framing this role wants; resume-consulting would under-sell the hands-on building the JD demands ("hands-on builder role", "show us what you've built").

