## Cover letter angle

Lendi's "we'd love to see what you've built" line means a generic PM cover letter is the weaker move. Options:
- **Preferred:** a short, specific note (Easy Apply has limited space anyway) that names two or three concrete agentic builds (the personal AI infrastructure that runs real workflows; AccessBuddy built solo at small-team velocity via AI-assisted development; the ANZ AI-enabled search APIs shipped to customers in a regulated bank) and offers to walk through them. This directly answers their hiring bias.
- If a full letter is wanted, use cover-letter-pm-base and follow "what sets me apart -> how I built it -> why this role", but bias every paragraph toward built proof, not capability claims.
- Style guardrails as always: no em dashes/semicolons/colons in prose, Australian English, no comparative language, exact closing line and sign-off.

## Positioning guardrails to honour

- **AccessBuddy:** for this role it can run hotter than the default supplementary framing (the JD is build-led), but never call it "commercial" or "paying". It is a solo, live, AI-leveraged build with a first tenant onboarded. Keep that exact framing.
- **LIMSOC:** "national scaling strategy" / "independent consulting piece" presented to the "national executive". Never "national product" or "CEO".
- **Seven** SLSA jurisdictions, not eight (and this CV currently gets it wrong - fix it).
- **No comparative language**, no "you are describing the work I already do" and cousins. State the build capability directly.
- AI claims must be backed: ANZ Vertex/Gemini AI-enabled search APIs, Claude Code early trial, responsible-AI guardrails are all in resume-swe/hybrid and are fair game. The personal-AI-infrastructure proof is real and Nick's to describe, just keep it concrete (workflows it runs) rather than grandiose.

## Portal questions / salary

- **Easy Apply screening:** expect short knockout questions (work rights, location/Melbourne or Sydney, years of experience, maybe "describe an AI agent you've built"). Be ready with a one-paragraph agentic-build answer.
- **Salary:** no band published. Melbourne APM in fintech typically lands ~$110k-$140k package. This is **below** Nick's ~$180k-$200k runway target, which is the crux of the level decision above. If asked, do not anchor to the APM band - frame against the value of the agentic-build capability and ask what range they hold for the role given the seniority of the work. If the band is firmly junior, that informs the apply/park call.

